Myth: Before the first dawn, the mortal realm was a dying husk, caught in the devastating crossfire between Aethelgard’s blazing sun-forge and the absolute zero of Vesper’s abyssal void. Knowing their eternal conflict would leave nothing but ashes and ice, the shadow dragon Vesper proposed a truce. He surrendered a single, frozen tear of pure darkness wrapped in shimmering shadow-silk, offering it to the celestial smith. Accepting this rare sacrifice, Aethelgard placed the tear upon his glowing anvil and struck it with a hammer forged from liquid dawn. From the explosive union of pure light and absolute dark emerged Lira—a colossal moth with wings of obsidian laced with glowing veins of spun silver. Breathed into life by both creators, Lira ascended to heal the fractured skies. To shield the world from the extremes of fire and frost, Lira endlessly weaves a massive, luminous chrysalis of starlight and shadow-silk. This celestial cocoon became the Moon. As she tirelessly spins, the Moon waxes into a brilliant sphere. When she unravels the threads to rest, it wanes to a delicate crescent. The dust shedding from her wings scatters across the night, crystallizing into stars—an eternal monument to the harmony of fire and shadow.
